`Art 33 the person may appeal the decision` interp appeal `the entity met the charity test at the time of the application` `the entity meets the charity test at the time of the appeal` `the name is undesirable under Article 12(1) at the time of the application` `the name is undesirable under Article 12(1) at the time of the appeal` MEANS
    NOT (`Art 33 the right of appeal` interp appeal `the entity met the charity test at the time of the application` `the entity meets the charity test at the time of the appeal` `the name is undesirable under Article 12(1) at the time of the application` `the name is undesirable under Article 12(1) at the time of the appeal` EQUALS `no appeal is given by Part 8`)

§§ `Article 34 -- Grounds of appeal and powers of tribunal in determining appeals`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 34(1): the ground
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `34(1) -- the ground of an appeal to the tribunal`

-- "wrong or unreasonable on its merits, the facts or the law": a decision said to be wrong, or said to be unreasonable, in at least one of those three respects.  Whether the decision IS wrong or
-- unreasonable is for the tribunal to find; this rule says what may be argued.  A complaint of some other kind (the appellant dislikes the result) is not a ground.
-- src:704 | 34 Grounds of appeal and powers of tribunal in determining appeals
-- src:705 | (1) An appeal may be made to the tribunal on the ground that the decision of the Commissioner was wrong or unreasonable on its merits, the facts or the law.
@ref Article 34(1) (src:705)
@export Whether the ground alleged is one Article 34(1) allows for an appeal to the Charity Tribunal: the decision of the Commissioner is wrong or unreasonable on its merits, the facts or the law
GIVEN ground IS A `Ground alleged in an appeal` @desc The complaint made against the decision: wrong or unreasonable, and in which respect
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 34(1) the appeal has a ground` ground MEANS
        (   ground's `standard alleged` EQUALS `wrong`
         OR ground's `standard alleged` EQUALS `unreasonable` )
    AND (   ground's `respect alleged` EQUALS `its merits`
         OR ground's `respect alleged` EQUALS `the facts`
         OR ground's `respect alleged` EQUALS `the law` )

