§§ `Article 35 -- Appeal and reference to court`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 35(1): who may appeal to the court, and on what ground
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `35(1) -- a party to the appeal, or the Attorney General, may appeal to the court`

-- F8.5.  Article 35(1) allows one ground: "that the decision was unreasonable having regard to all the circumstances of the case".  Reading A (taken): that is the only ground, so a decision that is
-- wrong but not unreasonable (wrong in law, say) cannot be appealed to the court on a party's appeal; an error of law reaches the court by the tribunal's reference (35(3)).
-- Reading B: the court appeal lies on any ground that Article 34(1) allows for the tribunal as well - wide enough to include a decision that is wrong in law.
-- src:713 | 35 Appeal and reference to court
-- src:714 | (1) A party to an appeal to the tribunal, or the Attorney General, may appeal from the decision of the tribunal to the court on the ground that the decision was unreasonable having regard to all the circumstances of the case.
@ref Article 35(1) (src:714)
GIVEN ground IS A `Ground alleged in an appeal` @desc The complaint made against the decision of the tribunal
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 35(1) reading A: the ground is that the decision was unreasonable` ground MEANS
    ground's `standard alleged` EQUALS `unreasonable`

@ref Article 35(1) (src:714)
GIVEN ground IS A `Ground alleged in an appeal` @desc The complaint made against the decision of the tribunal
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 35(1) reading B: the ground is one Article 34(1) allows or that the decision was unreasonable` ground MEANS
        `Art 34(1) the appeal has a ground` ground
    OR  `Art 35(1) reading A: the ground is that the decision was unreasonable` ground

@ref Article 35(1) (src:714)
@export Whether the person may appeal from the decision of the tribunal to the court: a party to the appeal to the tribunal, or the Attorney General, on the ground that the decision was unreasonable having regard to all the circumstances of the case (default - that is the only ground; with the other reading any ground Article 34(1) allows)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.5 is the one read here)
      `the person is a party to the appeal to the tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person was a party to the appeal to the tribunal
      `the person is the Attorney General` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is the Attorney General
      ground IS A `Ground alleged in an appeal` @desc The complaint made against the decision of the tribunal
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 35(1) the party may appeal to the court` interp `the person is a party to the appeal to the tribunal` `the person is the Attorney General` ground MEANS
        (   `the person is a party to the appeal to the tribunal`
         OR `the person is the Attorney General` )
    AND CONSIDER interp's `on what ground a decision of the tribunal is appealed to the court`
        WHEN `a decision of the tribunal is appealed to the court only on the ground in Article 35(1)` THEN `Art 35(1) reading A: the ground is that the decision was unreasonable` ground
        WHEN `a decision of the tribunal is appealed to the court on any ground in Article 34(1)` THEN `Art 35(1) reading B: the ground is one Article 34(1) allows or that the decision was unreasonable` ground

