§§ `Article 36 -- Further provision as to appeals to tribunal or court`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 36(1): the Commissioner's power to reconsider
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `36(1) -- nothing in Part 8 limits the Commissioner's power to reconsider`

-- The power is the Commissioner's own, whether a person asks or the Commissioner acts on the Commissioner's own motion, and Part 8 leaves it as it was: an appeal pending does not take it away.
-- (The rule reads no fact about an appeal, so it stands whatever Part 8 says.)  The decisions are the Commissioner's own or those of the Commissioner's staff.
-- src:719 | (1) Nothing in this Part is to be read as limiting the Commissioner’s power to reconsider his or her own decisions or the decisions of his or her staff, whether at the request of any person or on the Commissioner’s own motion.
@ref Article 36(1) (src:719)
@export The Commissioner's power to reconsider the Commissioner's own decisions or the decisions of the Commissioner's staff, whether at the request of any person or on the Commissioner's own motion; Part 8 does not limit it. FULFILLED where the decision is neither
GIVEN `the decision is a decision of the Commissioner or of the Commissioner's staff` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision to reconsider is one of the Commissioner's own or of a member of the Commissioner's staff
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 32 to 36`
`Art 36(1) the Commissioner may reconsider a decision` `the decision is a decision of the Commissioner or of the Commissioner's staff` MEANS
    IF   `the decision is a decision of the Commissioner or of the Commissioner's staff`
    THEN PARTY `the Commissioner`
         MAY `reconsider a decision of the Commissioner or of the Commissioner's staff`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 36(2): the Minister's Order on time limits and notice
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `36(2) -- the Minister may prescribe time limits and provide for notice of decisions`

-- src:720 | (2) The Minister may by Order –
-- src:721 | (a) prescribe time limits within which appeals to the tribunal or court are to be brought; and
-- src:722 | (b) make such provision as to the giving of notice of decisions by the Commissioner or the tribunal, or of notice of reasons for such decisions, as appears to the Minister to be expedient for the purpose of enabling appeals to be brought within any time limits.
@ref Article 36(2)(a) (src:721)
@export The Minister's power to prescribe, by Order, time limits within which appeals to the tribunal or court are to be brought; the content of the Order is an input read by Art 36(2) the appeal is in time
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 32 to 36`
`Art 36(2) the Minister may by Order set time limits` MEANS
    PARTY `the Minister`
    MAY `make an Order prescribing time limits for appeals`

@ref Article 36(2)(b) (src:722)
@export The Minister's power to provide, by Order, for the giving of notice of decisions of the Commissioner or the tribunal, or of notice of reasons for them, as appears to the Minister to be expedient for enabling appeals to be brought within any time limits; FULFILLED where it does not so appear
GIVEN `it appears to the Minister to be expedient for the purpose of enabling appeals to be brought within any time limits` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The Minister's appearance is an input
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 32 to 36`
`Art 36(2)(b) the Minister may by Order provide for notice of decisions and of reasons` `it appears to the Minister to be expedient for the purpose of enabling appeals to be brought within any time limits` MEANS
    IF   `it appears to the Minister to be expedient for the purpose of enabling appeals to be brought within any time limits`
    THEN PARTY `the Minister`
         MAY `make an Order providing for notice of decisions and of reasons for them`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- P27, F8.6, F8.12.  The Law prescribes no time limit; an Order may.  The Order is not among the sources, so the limit is an input.  What a limit runs from is not said either: reading A (taken) runs it from the
-- notice of the decision (36(2)(b) provides for notice "for the purpose of enabling appeals to be brought within any time limits"), reading B from the decision.  Counting: reading A (taken) does not count the
-- day it runs from, so a limit of 30 days from 10 January ends on 9 February; reading B counts it as the first day.  An appeal brought on the last day is in time.
@ref Article 36(2)(a) and (b) (src:721-722)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.6 is the one read here)
      dates IS A `Dates of an appeal under Article 36(2)` @desc The day of the decision, the day notice of it was given (if it was), and the day the appeal was lodged
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 36(2) the day the time limit runs from` interp dates MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `from when an appeal time limit under Article 36(2) runs`
    WHEN `an appeal time limit under Article 36(2) runs from the notice of the decision` THEN
        CONSIDER dates's `day of the notice of the decision`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN REFUSE "notice of the decision has not been given, and on this reading the time limit runs from the notice"
        WHEN JUST noticed THEN noticed
    WHEN `an appeal time limit under Article 36(2) runs from the decision` THEN dates's `day of the decision`

@ref Article 36(2)(a) (src:721)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.12 is the one read here)
      anchor IS A DATE @desc The day the time limit runs from
      days IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the Order allows
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 36(2)(a) the last day of the time limit` interp anchor days MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `whether the first day counts in an appeal time limit under Article 36(2)`
    WHEN `the day of the anchor is not counted in an appeal time limit` THEN anchor PLUS days
    WHEN `the day of the anchor is the first day of an appeal time limit` THEN anchor PLUS (days MINUS 1)

@ref Article 36(2) (src:720-722)
@export The last day on which an appeal may be brought, under a time limit of the given number of days prescribed by an Order under Article 36(2)(a): the limit runs from the notice of the decision (default) or from the decision, and the first day is not counted (default) or is; refuses if the limit runs from a notice that has not been given
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.6 and F8.12 are the ones read here)
      dates IS A `Dates of an appeal under Article 36(2)` @desc The day of the decision, the day notice of it was given (if it was), and the day the appeal was lodged
      days IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the Order allows
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 36(2) the last day to bring the appeal` interp dates days MEANS
    `Art 36(2)(a) the last day of the time limit` interp (`Art 36(2) the day the time limit runs from` interp dates) days

@ref Article 36(2) (src:720-722)
@export Whether the appeal was brought in time under a time limit prescribed by Order under Article 36(2)(a): on or before the last day; TRUE where no Order has prescribed a limit; refuses where the Order is not supplied, and where the limit runs from a notice that has not been given
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.6 and F8.12 are the ones read here)
      dates IS A `Dates of an appeal under Article 36(2)` @desc The day of the decision, the day notice of it was given (if it was), and the day the appeal was lodged
      limit IS A MAYBE `Time limit prescribed under Article 36(2)(a)` @desc The time limit an Order prescribes; NOTHING if the Order is not supplied (its content is not in the sources)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 36(2) the appeal is in time` interp dates limit MEANS
    CONSIDER limit
    WHEN NOTHING THEN REFUSE "no Order under Article 36(2)(a) is supplied, and none is among the sources, so whether an appeal is in time is not answered"
    WHEN JUST prescribed THEN
        CONSIDER prescribed
        WHEN `no Order has prescribed a time limit` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `an Order prescribes a time limit of days` n THEN dates's `day the appeal was lodged` AT MOST `Art 36(2) the last day to bring the appeal` interp dates n

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 36(3), 36(4): a substituted decision that takes effect from another date
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `36(3) -- a substituted decision may take effect from any appropriate date`

-- The court or the tribunal that substitutes its decision may order it to take effect "for all purposes from any date appearing to the court or tribunal to be appropriate, whether that date is before,
-- on or after the date of the original decision or of the court's or tribunal's own decision".  The appearance is an input; no date is excluded.
-- src:723 | (3) If the court or tribunal substitutes its decision for any other decision, it may order that the substituted decision is to take effect for all purposes from any date appearing to the court or tribunal to be appropriate, whether that date is before, on or after the date of the original decision or of the court’s or tribunal’s own decision.
@ref Article 36(3) (src:723)
@export The power of the court or the tribunal, when it substitutes its decision for another, to order that the substituted decision takes effect for all purposes from any date that appears to it to be appropriate; FULFILLED where it does not substitute a decision or the date does not so appear, or where the actor is neither
GIVEN `the court or the tribunal` IS A `Legal actor` @desc Who is deciding: the court or the Charity Tribunal
      `it substitutes its decision for another decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court or tribunal substitutes its decision for any other decision
      `the date appears to it to be appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The court's or the tribunal's view of the date; it may be before, on or after either decision
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 32 to 36`
`Art 36(3) the court or tribunal may order the date from which the substituted decision takes effect` `the court or the tribunal` `it substitutes its decision for another decision` `the date appears to it to be appropriate` MEANS
    IF       (   `the court or the tribunal` EQUALS `the court`
              OR `the court or the tribunal` EQUALS `the Charity Tribunal` )
         AND `it substitutes its decision for another decision`
         AND `the date appears to it to be appropriate`
    THEN PARTY `the court or the tribunal`
         MAY `order that a substituted decision takes effect from a date`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- When no date is ordered the Law does not say when the substituted decision takes effect: a named refusal.  When one is, it is that date, whether before, on or after the dates of the decisions.
@ref Article 36(3) (src:723)
@export The date from which a substituted decision takes effect: the date the court or tribunal ordered; refuses where none was ordered, because Article 36(3) does not say
GIVEN `the date ordered` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc NOTHING if the court or tribunal ordered no date
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 36(3) the date from which the substituted decision takes effect` `the date ordered` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the date ordered`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN REFUSE "Article 36(3) does not say when a substituted decision takes effect if no date is ordered"
    WHEN JUST ordered THEN ordered

@ref Article 36(3) (src:723)
GIVEN `the date ordered` IS A DATE @desc The date from which the substituted decision is to take effect
      `the date of the order` IS A DATE @desc The date of the order of the court or tribunal
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 36(3) the substituted decision takes effect from a date before the date of the order` `the date ordered` `the date of the order` MEANS
    `the date ordered` LESS THAN `the date of the order`

§§§ `36(4) -- an act before the date of the order is not rendered an offence merely by the order`

-- The parallel of Article 17(4) (G6).  An act that occurred before the date of the order is not rendered an offence merely by virtue of the operation of the order, when the order makes the substituted
-- decision take effect from a date before the date of the order.  An act that is an offence apart from the order stays one.
-- src:724 | (4) If the court or tribunal makes an order under paragraph (3) that a substituted decision is to take effect from a date before the date of the order, an act, that occurred before the date of the order, is not rendered an offence merely by virtue of the operation of the order.
@ref Article 36(4) (src:724)
GIVEN `the substituted decision takes effect from a date before the date of the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The result of Art 36(3) the substituted decision takes effect from a date before the date of the order
      `the act occurred before the date of the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act occurred before the date of the order
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 36(4) the act is protected from the operation of the order` `the substituted decision takes effect from a date before the date of the order` `the act occurred before the date of the order` MEANS
        `the substituted decision takes effect from a date before the date of the order`
    AND `the act occurred before the date of the order`

@ref Article 36(4) (src:724)
@export Whether the act is an offence in the light of an order under Article 36(3): it is if it is an offence apart from the order, or if it is made one only by the operation of the order and Article 36(4) does not protect it
GIVEN `the substituted decision takes effect from a date before the date of the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The result of Art 36(3) the substituted decision takes effect from a date before the date of the order
      `the act occurred before the date of the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act occurred before the date of the order
      `the act is an offence apart from the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act is an offence independently of the order's retrospective operation
      `the act is an offence only by the operation of the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order's retrospective operation is all that makes the act an offence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 36(4) the act is an offence in the light of the order` `the substituted decision takes effect from a date before the date of the order` `the act occurred before the date of the order` `the act is an offence apart from the order` `the act is an offence only by the operation of the order` MEANS
        `the act is an offence apart from the order`
    OR  (    `the act is an offence only by the operation of the order`
         AND NOT `Art 36(4) the act is protected from the operation of the order` `the substituted decision takes effect from a date before the date of the order` `the act occurred before the date of the order` )
